WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:07.400
Today you will build a weekly dashboard that does its own sums, and you will learn to prove that every number is right.

2
00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:11.960
In Level 1 you used SUMIF for one condition, like one region.

3
00:00:11.960 --> 00:00:23.980
SUMIFS and COUNTIFS check several conditions at once, such as this week, delivered only, and paid on credit.

4
00:00:23.980 --> 00:00:26.900
You describe the conditions in plain words, and the AI writes the formula.

5
00:00:26.900 --> 00:00:31.820
The AI can write cleaning formulas for you.

6
00:00:31.820 --> 00:00:40.130
TRIM removes extra spaces, and a check column shows OK or Check when an amount is not quantity times rate.

7
00:00:40.130 --> 00:00:45.390
Clean the data first, or every total after it will be wrong.

8
00:00:45.390 --> 00:00:50.400
Put the week start date in one cell, and point every formula at that cell.

9
00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:55.070
Change the date or add new rows, and the whole dashboard recalculates.

10
00:00:55.070 --> 00:01:02.790
The TEXT function turns dates and amounts into neat labels, such as Week of 19 Oct.

11
00:01:02.790 --> 00:01:09.470
Conditional formatting colours a cell automatically when a rule is true, such as red when sales are below target.

12
00:01:09.470 --> 00:01:14.330
The AI can write the rule for you.

13
00:01:14.330 --> 00:01:20.570
Colour helps you notice a problem, but you still decide what to do.

14
00:01:20.570 --> 00:01:25.920
A formula can look perfect and still be wrong by one day or one condition.

15
00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:30.490
Check one number yourself with a filter and compare it with the dashboard.

16
00:01:30.490 --> 00:01:37.550
If they differ, find out why before you share anything.

17
00:01:37.550 --> 00:01:39.810
We upload the practice orders file and ask Claude what would make our weekly totals wrong.

18
00:01:39.810 --> 00:01:45.690
It finds a duplicate order, extra spaces, amounts typed as text, and one blank amount.

19
00:01:45.690 --> 00:01:52.660
Best of all, it suggests a check column that flags every row where the amount does not add up.

20
00:01:52.660 --> 00:01:59.220
We fix each problem ourselves in the sheet, until every row in the check column says OK.

21
00:01:59.220 --> 00:02:05.950
Here is the whole dashboard, and every formula points at one cell, B1, the week start date.

22
00:02:05.950 --> 00:02:12.750
SUMIFS adds the amounts for this week and delivered orders only, and COUNTIFS counts them.

23
00:02:12.750 --> 00:02:19.160
The credit formula adds one more condition, so it only counts orders paid on credit.

24
00:02:19.160 --> 00:02:26.660
For the week of nineteenth October, delivered sales are fifteen thousand, three hundred and ten rupees.

25
00:02:26.660 --> 00:02:34.930
Now we ask Gemini for the weekly sales formula, without saying exactly where the week ends.

26
00:02:34.930 --> 00:02:41.610
The formula looks right, and it gives sixteen thousand, five hundred and thirty rupees.

27
00:02:41.610 --> 00:02:47.560
But our filter check, for nineteenth to twenty-fifth October, says fifteen thousand, three hundred and ten.

28
00:02:47.560 --> 00:02:56.150
The less-than-or-equal sign also counted next Monday, so we change it to less than, and the numbers match.

29
00:02:56.150 --> 00:03:03.450
Conditional formatting turns a number into a signal, green for on target and red for below.

30
00:03:03.450 --> 00:03:09.650
The dollar sign in the row rule keeps the colour on the whole row, not just one cell.

31
00:03:09.650 --> 00:03:15.400
Now change the week date, and watch every number and colour update by itself.

32
00:03:15.400 --> 00:03:22.230
Finally, check one total with a filter, because a dashboard is only useful when it is right.

33
00:03:22.230 --> 00:03:25.690
Great work! Let’s remember what we learned.

34
00:03:25.690 --> 00:03:31.170
Now build along, then try the challenges. You’ve got this!
